-- The questions the semantic fixture asks, of a file that is halfway through being edited.
--
-- What M7 buys is that this file has answers at all: the declarations below a broken one still
-- parse, so the index still holds them, and the resolver says nothing further about what the
-- parser already reported. The diagnostics section at the top of the .expect is the second half of
-- that - one message per mistake, and every one of them from the parser.

module

import Shapes

-- A function whose body has not been typed yet.
fn halfWritten(s: Shape) -> Int:

-- A `match` whose alternatives have not been typed yet. The pivot is still what an editor is asked
-- about while they are being written.
fn pending(s: Shape) -> Int = match s:
--                                  ^pivot

-- A declaration that got as far as its name.
data Colour

-- A binding whose `| match:` alternatives have not been typed yet. Without the poisoned
-- alternative the parser writes in their place, the resolver would tell a half-typed line that its
-- refutable pattern needs the alternatives that are being typed.
fn probe(s: Shape) -> Int:
    let Circle(c) = s | match:

    return c.radius
--         ^bound

-- Everything after all of that still resolves.
fn twice(s: Shape) -> Int = area(s) + area(s)
--                          ^classfun

-- A call whose arguments have not been typed yet, which is the state every call passes through and
-- the one an argument overlay exists for. There is no node covering the cursor here at all - the
-- bracket is where the parse stopped - so the answer comes from the text rather than from the
-- index; before it did, this position answered nothing, which is to say the overlay never appeared
-- while a call was being written.
fn typing(s: Shape) -> Int:
    return twice(
--               ^sigTyping

-- A named argument, which is where the overlay's own answer stops being the comma count: `by` is the
-- second parameter of `scale`, so the highlight follows the name to it rather than staying on the
-- first. The name is read out of the text for the same reason the bracket is - there is no argument
-- node here to have a name.
fn naming(s: Shape) -> Int:
    return Shapes.scale(by:
--                         ^sigNamed

-- And the name written second, filling the parameter declared first: the highlight goes back to it
-- rather than forward with the comma.
fn namingBack(s: Shape) -> Int:
    return Shapes.scale(2, s:
--                           ^sigNamedBack

fn main() -> Int:
    let c = Shapes.Circle {radius: 4}
--                 ^con
    return twice(c)
--         ^call
--               ^sigWritten
